# Reverse proxy routes — spec §5.2.
#
# Shared by both the production and dev Caddyfiles so the two can never drift.
# The only difference between environments is PUBLIC_DOMAIN and how certificates
# are issued; the routing is identical.
#
#   production   PUBLIC_DOMAIN=media.thorby.tech   certs from Let's Encrypt
#   development  PUBLIC_DOMAIN=<ip>.sslip.io       certs from Caddy's internal CA
#
# Upstreams are container names on the compose bridge network, so these must
# stay in step with `container_name` in docker-compose.yml.
#
# ── Only three services are routed here, and that is the whole point ──────────
#
# Sonarr, Radarr, Prowlarr, Bazarr, qBittorrent and SABnzbd are deliberately
# absent. They are reached at <lan-ip>:<port> on the LAN, or over Tailscale from
# anywhere — never through this proxy and never under a public name. qBittorrent
# runs an external program on completion and SABnzbd runs post-processing
# scripts; both are arbitrary command execution by design (spec §5.3), so the
# exclusion is structural rather than a matter of remembering.
#
# Do not add them back "temporarily", and do not leave a commented-out block
# here — a commented route is one keystroke from being live. scripts/validate.sh
# fails if any of those six names appears in this file at all (decisions.md D25).

# Every site imports `common` — access logging plus baseline security headers.
# It is defined in Caddyfile and Caddyfile.dev rather than here, because the two
# environments need different headers: HSTS is right on a real domain and wrong
# on a dev CA. Routing stays shared; anything that legitimately differs per
# environment lives in the two Caddyfiles, same as certificate issuance does.

# The front door. Static files, served by this same Caddy — no extra container.
#
# `private_only` tags requests coming from the LAN or the tailnet, and
# `templates` lets index.html use that tag to decide whether to render the
# Manage section. Both are required: without `templates` the conditional would
# render as literal text *and* the admin links would appear. validate.sh
# asserts it.
{$PUBLIC_DOMAIN} {
	import common
	import private_only
	root * /srv/site
	templates
	file_server
}

jellyfin.{$PUBLIC_DOMAIN} {
	import common
	reverse_proxy jellyfin:8096
}

# Requests — where everyone who is not the administrator should start.
seerr.{$PUBLIC_DOMAIN} {
	import common
	reverse_proxy jellyseerr:5055
}
